    "exercises": [
      "Added reference explanation. The hypothesis makes P(5)P(5)P(5) and P(9)P(9)P(9) available, since 2≤5,9<452\\leq5,9<452≤5,9<45. A finite prime product for five and one for nine combine into a finite prime product for forty-five. P(44)P(44)P(44) alone does not supply these two factorizations, and P(45)P(45)P(45) cannot be assumed within this step.",
      "Added reference explanation. One lies outside the domain m≥2m\\geq2m≥2, and the other factor is nnn itself, outside the smaller range m<nm<nm<n. Using P(n)P(n)P(n) to establish P(n)P(n)P(n) would be circular. Nontrivial factors a,b≥2a,b\\geq2a,b≥2 instead guarantee 2≤a,b<n2\\leq a,b<n2≤a,b<n.",
      "Added reference explanation. No. The argument constructs at least one finite prime product for each integer n≥2n\\geq2n≥2. It does not compare arbitrary prime products for the same integer. Uniqueness, apart from rearranging the factors, needs a separate general proof; agreement for sixty is only an example."
